==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|en|seneskal||dated or rare}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|seneschal}} (recorded in English since 1393), from {{der|en|fro|seneschal}}, from {{der|en|ML.|siniscalcus}}, from {{der|en|frk|*siniskalk}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*siniskalkaz}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*siniz||senior}} + {{m|gem-pro|*skalkaz||servant}}; latter term as in {{m|en|marshal}}. As an officer of the French crown, via {{der|en|fr|sénéchal}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|sĕnʹə-shəl}} {{IPA|en|/ˈsɛnəʃəl/}}
* {{hyph|en|sen|e|schal}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[steward]], [[particularly]] {{lb|en|historical}} one [[in charge]] of a [[medieval]] [[nobleman]]'s [[estate]].
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|page=131|volume=I|text=Beside stood '''seneschals''', the appointed witnesses of the ensuing games.}}
#* {{RQ:Twain Huckleberry Finn|chapter=35|passage={{...}} so the very keenest '''seneskal''' can't see no sign {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|historical}} An [[officer]] of the [[crown]] in [[late]] [[medieval]] and [[early modern]] [[France]] who [[serve]]d as a [[kind]] of [[governor]] and [[chief]] [[justice]] of the [[royal]] [[court]] in [[Normandy]] and [[Languedoc]].

